/** @mainpage

This suite audits a single-node Kubernetes cluster (k3s, brought up in
Docker on the Test Agent it runs on) the way an unauthenticated network
peer would see it:

- @ref k8s-group - three audits, each run both against the secure
  cluster (expected to pass) and against a reversible known-bad control
  (expected to fail, recorded in the TRC):
  - @c anon_api - anonymous reads of the API server (secrets, pods,
    namespaces, the RBAC bindings); the control binds @c cluster-admin
    to @c system:anonymous;
  - @c rbac - what the authorizer grants @c system:anonymous, read with
    @c kubectl @c auth @c can-i; the same control;
  - @c anon_kubelet - unauthenticated reads of the kubelet (@c /pods,
    @c /runningpods, @c /metrics); the control is a throwaway cluster
    whose kubelet runs with anonymous auth on.

Findings are collected through tsf-cybersec and the gate fails on HIGH.
Every audit emits an MI line-graph of the anonymous HTTP status (or the
allowed/denied answer) per endpoint, so the HTML log draws the posture.

The cluster is the suite's to bring up and tear down; with no Docker the
group skips cleanly. The audits read only - authorized use only.

*/
